Measures for the Selection and Appointment of Presiding Judges of People's Courts (for Trial Implementation)

(Adopted by the Judicial Committee of the Supreme People's Court at its 1123rd Session on July 11, 2000)

Fa Fa [2000] No. 16

In order to improve the quality of the judge contingent, give full play to the function of the collegial panel, ensure judicial impartiality, and enhance trial efficiency, these Measures are formulated in accordance with the Organic Law of the People's Courts of the People's Republic of China, the Judges Law of the People's Republic of China, and relevant legal provisions, and in light of trial practice.

1. Principles for Selection and Appointment

(1) Implementation according to law;
(2) Both moral integrity and professional competence;
(3) Openness, equality, competition, and择优 selection;
(4) Dynamic management, survival of the fittest;
(5) Adherence to democratic centralism.

2. Staffing of Presiding Judges

The number of presiding judges to be staffed in people's courts at each level shall be determined based on the needs of trial work and with reference to the number of collegial panels in the respective court. The number of presiding judges to be staffed in the Supreme People's Court shall be determined by the Supreme People's Court. The number of presiding judges to be staffed in local people's courts shall be determined by the higher people's courts.

Presiding judges shall generally be assumed by judges. If an outstanding assistant judge is selected as a presiding judge, he or she shall, in accordance with the law, be recommended for appointment as a judge.

When the president, vice president, members of the judicial committee, division chief, or deputy division chief participate in the trial of a case as part of a collegial panel, they shall serve as the presiding judge in accordance with legal provisions.

3. Conditions for Presiding Judges

To serve as a presiding judge, the following conditions shall be met:

(1) Abide by the Constitution and laws, strictly observe trial discipline, enforce the law impartially, be honest and clean, and possess good professional ethics.
(2) Be in good health and capable of performing trial work.
(3) Presiding judges of the Supreme People's Court and higher people's courts shall generally possess a bachelor's degree or above in law from a institution of higher education; presiding judges of intermediate people's courts shall generally possess a bachelor's degree or above in law from a institution of higher education; presiding judges of primary people's courts shall generally possess a junior college degree or above in law from a institution of higher education.
(4) Presiding judges of the Supreme People's Court and higher people's courts must have served as judges and engaged in trial work for at least 5 years; presiding judges of intermediate people's courts must have served as judges and engaged in trial work for at least 4 years; presiding judges of primary people's courts must have served as judges and engaged in trial work for at least 3 years.
(5) Possess relatively rich practical experience in trial work, be able to apply the legal expertise acquired to solve practical problems in trial work; be able to skillfully preside over court proceedings; and possess strong oral and written expression skills, and be able to draft procedural documents in a standardized and proficient manner.

People's courts in economically and culturally underdeveloped regions may, upon deliberation and decision by the judicial committee of the respective court and with approval from the next higher people's court, appropriately relax the educational requirements and the number of years engaged in trial work for presiding judges.

4. Selection and Appointment Procedures

The selection and appointment of presiding judges shall follow the following procedures:

(1) Announce the number of vacancies for presiding judges and the requirements;
(2) Judges who meet the conditions may submit a written application, or the division chief or the responsible president may recommend candidates from among judges who meet the conditions;
(3) Conduct a preliminary review of the qualifications of self-recommended and recommended persons based on the selection conditions, determine a list of pre-selected candidates, and publicly announce it;
(4) Conduct trial business examinations and assessments of the pre-selected candidates;
(5) The judicial committee shall comprehensively consider the selection conditions and the results of the examinations and assessments, determine the list of appointees, and the president shall announce it.

5. Duties

The duties of a presiding judge are:

(1) Serve as the case undertaker, or designate another member of the collegial panel to serve as the case undertaker;
(2) Organize the members of the collegial panel and relevant personnel to make preparations for the trial and related work;
(3) Preside over court proceedings;
(4) Preside over the collegial panel's deliberation on the case and render a judgment;
(5) For major, difficult, and complex cases, and cases where there is a significant divergence of opinions within the collegial panel, report to the president in accordance with the prescribed procedures for submission to the judicial committee for deliberation and decision;
(6) Review and sign procedural documents in accordance with the prescribed authority;
(7) Complete other trial work in accordance with the law.

6. Management and Supervision

Dynamic management shall be implemented for presiding judges.

A case evaluation system shall be established to conduct focused evaluations and random sampling evaluations of cases tried by collegial panels. The results of the evaluations shall serve as an important component of the assessment of presiding judges.

Annual assessments shall be conducted for presiding judges. On the basis of a comprehensive assessment, emphasis shall be placed on the assessment of actual trial work performance. If the annual assessment is unqualified, the presiding judge shall be removed from the position.

7. Removal and Discipline

If a presiding judge, during his or her term of office, falls under any of the following circumstances, he or she shall be removed from the position of presiding judge:

(1) Engaging in illegal adjudication;
(2) Receiving Party discipline or administrative sanctions;
(3) Being unable to continue serving as presiding judge due to health reasons;
(4) Submitting a resignation that has been approved;
(5) Being transferred from the trial work post;
(6) Being removed from the position of judge in accordance with the law;
(7) Other circumstances where it is inappropriate to serve as presiding judge.

If a presiding judge is removed from the position due to illegal adjudication, responsibility shall be pursued in accordance with the Measures for the Investigation of Responsibility for Illegal Adjudication by Judicial Personnel of the People's Courts (for Trial Implementation) and the Measures for the Imposition of Disciplinary Sanctions on Judicial Personnel of the People's Courts (for Trial Implementation).

The removal of a presiding judge shall be reported by the division chief to the president, who shall submit it to the judicial committee for a decision, and the president shall announce it.

8. Benefits

Presiding judges may receive special allowances.

9. Supplementary Provisions

All higher people's courts shall, in accordance with the relevant provisions of these Measures and in light of the actual conditions of their respective regions, formulate specific implementation rules.

July 28, 2000
